From 0556e8ad1ccd6a9a3aeccd313670f1293594d6e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Proofloom Contributor <proofloom-contributor@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 05:02:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Accept inflections and declensions of known words in every
 language
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Unknown words no longer become spelling errors when they decompose
into a known root plus a plausible ending for the document language,
and when nothing else fits, suggestions offer the main root form
(사랑은의 -> 사랑). This covers wordlist-only dictionaries, personal
lists, imported packs, and the gaps inside bundled affix tables —
dictionary-ko lists verb conjugations but no nominal particles, so
사랑은 used to be flagged even though 사랑 is a dictionary word.

- src/core/data/inflections.js: per-language inflection tables —
  suffix strips (case endings, plural markers, verb endings), ending
  swaps for velar/y/umlaut alternations (книги -> книга, studies ->
  study, wolves -> wolf), and clitic prefixes (Arabic وال -> ...),
  with curated tables for ~40 languages and aliases onto close
  relatives (nds -> de, af -> nl, sr-latn -> hr, ckb -> ar, ...).
  Languages without a curated table fall back by script (Latin,
  Cyrillic, Arabic, Devanagari, Hangul, Greek, Hebrew, Georgian), so
  the mechanism reaches all 86 bundled languages and user packs.
- src/core/morphology.js: bounded analyzer (max depth per language,
  24-stem frontier, LRU cache in the engine) with false-positive
  controls: function-word guards ("thes", "les"), doubled-boundary
  rejection ("walkss"), strict -es only after sibilants/o ("walkes"
  stays wrong), consonant+y -> -ies/-ied ("studys"), silent-e rules
  ("makeing", "makeed" stay wrong; "making", "loved" resolve), and
  a verb gate that rejects un-doubled stems when the bare stem is
  itself a word (run -> running, so "runing" stays flagged). Verb
  endings attach only at depth one; English stays single-step.
- src/core/checker.js: correct() consults the analyzer after every
  dictionary has rejected a word; suggest() falls back to the root
  form when no other candidate exists. Always-on: rich dictionaries
  accept their inflections through their own affix data first, so
  the analyzer only ever sees words every dictionary rejected.
- tests/morphology.test.mjs: 11 tests — variant/alias/script
  resolution for 80 language codes, root resolution for English,
  German, Turkish, Hungarian, Russian, Korean, Arabic, Hindi,
  Spanish and Dutch chains, typo look-alike rejection, wordlist-only
  and bundled-dictionary engines, root-form suggestions, and a
  bounded-analysis loop.

npm test: 128 passing. npm run build unchanged at 86 dictionaries.
